# %r3 < 32 guards both stores, %r3 written under a guard on the
# parameter n. In the first kernel %r3 is 100 unless n is 0, so no
# thread stores unless n is 0; in the second both writes are %tid.x and
# 32 threads store. A selector's operands are read strictly, a write
# under a guard refused, so each store is a bound, all 64 threads.
RUN: %ptxroof analyze %fixtures/micro/guarded_selector.ptx | FileCheck "%s"
CHECK: kernel micro_guarded_selector
CHECK: st.global.u32  global store 4 B (predicated)  warp: <= 5 sectors (ideal 4)  [4 * %tid.x + param_0]
CHECK: totals per CTA [static]:
CHECK: global bytes: load 0 B, store <= 256 B
CHECK: kernel micro_guarded_selector_agreeing
CHECK: st.global.u32  global store 4 B (predicated)  warp: <= 5 sectors (ideal 4)  [4 * %tid.x + param_0]
CHECK: totals per CTA [static]:
CHECK: global bytes: load 0 B, store <= 256 B
